t \\(U\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of uncovered unit squares such that\n\n\\[\n\\forall i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\;\\exists!\\;j\\;(i,j)\\in U,\\qquad\n\\forall j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\;\\exists!\\;i\\;(i,j)\\in U .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(U\\) is a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(M\\) the \\(n\\times n\\) binary matrix defined by  \n\n\\[\nM_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n0,& (i,j)\\in U,\\\\[2pt]\n1,& (i,j)\\notin U .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(M=J-P\\), where \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix and \\(P\\) is the permutation matrix of \\(\\pi\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound \\(N_{\\min}\\le n\\)\n\nFix the permutation \\(\\pi\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place a horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq \\pi(i)\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the whole row except the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_n\\) are pairwise disjoint, each covers only squares with entry \\(1\\) in \\(M\\), and together they cover all such squares.  \nThus a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists, proving  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\min}\\le n=2025.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lower bound \\(N_{\\min}\\ge n\\)\n\nSuppose a tiling uses \\(r\\) rectangles.  \nFor a rectangle \\(R\\) let \\(a\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) be its row‑indicator vector ( \\(a_i=1\\) iff row \\(i\\) belongs to \\(R\\) ) and let \\(b\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) be its column‑indicator vector.  \nThe characteristic matrix of \\(R\\) is the outer product \\(ab^{\\mathsf T}\\), which has rank \\(1\\) over the real numbers.\n\nSince the rectangles are disjoint and together cover precisely the entries equal to \\(1\\) in \\(M\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nM=\\sum_{k=1}^{r} a^{(k)}\\bigl(b^{(k)}\\bigr)^{\\mathsf T},\n\\tag{6}\n