utation. This is known as the \"covering number\" of the complement of a permutation matrix by combinatorial rectangles (i.e., submatrices). This is related to the \"rectangle covering number\" of a matrix, which is the minimum number of monochromatic rectangles needed to cover all 1-entries of a matrix. For a permutation matrix (with zeros on the permutation positions and ones elsewhere), the rectangle covering number is known to be n (I think). Actually the rectangle covering number of the complement of a permutation matrix may be n. Let's recall known results: The Boolean rank (or rectangle covering number) of the complement of a permutation matrix is n. The Boolean rank of the matrix J-P is n. Indeed, the rank over reals is n, but the Boolean rank may be larger. However, the problem is about covering with rectangles that are axis-aligned contiguous blocks (i.e., submatrices). That's exactly the rectangle covering number. The rank argument gave a lower bound of n for real rank, but the rectangle covering number can be larger than real rank. However, the solution uses rank argument to claim lower bound r ≥ n. That's correct because each rectangle corresponds to a rank-1 matrix, but the sum of rank-1 matrices can have rank up to r, but the rank of the sum can be less than r. Actually rank(M) ≤ r, so r ≥ rank(M). So lower bound r ≥ n is correct. So the minimal number of rectangles is at least n. The question: can we achieve exactly n? The solution's construction fails. But maybe there is another construction achieving n. Let's think about known results: The Boolean rank of J-P (the complement of a permutation matrix) is exactly n. I recall that the Boolean rank (or rectangle covering number) of the complement of a permutation matrix is n. Actually, the Boolean rank of the matrix J-P is exactly n. This is known: the Boolean rank of the all-ones matrix J is 1, but J-P has Boolean rank n. Indeed, there is a known result: The Boolean rank of a matrix is the minimum numb