ruction is valid. The lower bound argument: They define left-type and right-type tiles based on whether holes are to the right or left of the tile. They claim each left-type tile has a distinct rightmost column R(T). They argue that if two left-type tiles share same R, they could be merged into a larger rectangle, contradicting maximality of a tiling. Need to examine if this argument is sound: The tiling is not required to be maximal in any sense; they are just constructing a lower bound for any admissible tiling. They assume that a tiling can be assumed to be maximal in the sense that you cannot merge two left-type tiles with same R without covering a hole? Let's examine.\n\nThe argument: For a left-type tile T, all rows covered by T have holes to the right of the tile. So the tile occupies columns from L(T) to R(T) inclusive, and rows some interval. If there are two left-type tiles T1 and T2 with same R, they claim their row intervals could be merged into a single larger rectangle (still left-type), contradicting maximality of a tiling. But is it guaranteed that the row intervals are contiguous? The tiles are rectangles, so each tile's rows are a contiguous interval. If two left-type tiles have same R but possibly overlapping rows? Actually they would be disjoint because each row can have only one tile covering it (since each unit square can be covered at most once, but a row could have multiple tiles covering different columns). However, if two left-type tiles have same R, they could be in different columns (i.e., L values differ) but share same rightmost column. For each row, there is exactly one uncovered cell, but multiple tiles could occupy the same row in disjoint columns. For left-type tiles, they occupy columns up to R, and holes are to the right. So for a given row i, the tile covering that row must have its rightmost column less than the hole column pi(i). So each row i can be covered by at most one left-type tile, because if there were two left-type tiles covering the same row, they'd overlap (since they both occupy some columns). But they could be in same row but different columns? Actually a row can have multiple tiles covering different columns, as long as they don't overlap. For left-type tiles, each tile occupies a contiguous block of columns from L to R. If you have two left-type tiles in the same row, they'd have to be in disjoint column intervals. But they both must have holes to the right of the tile. If they share same R, they'd have overlapping column intervals at column R, causing overlap. So they cannot share same row. So left-type tiles with same R must have disjoint row intervals. Then merging them into a single rectangle covering the union of rows and same column interval would be a rectangle covering all rows between min row and max row, but there may be gaps (rows not covered by either tile). However, if there are gaps, merging would create a rectangle covering rows that might include a hole (uncovered cell) because some rows in the gap might have holes that lie to the left of the tile? Actually for left-type tiles, holes are to the right of the tile, so for any row i in the gap, the hole is to the right of column R, which is fine. So the merged rectangle would still be left-type (holes to the right). But does the merged rectangle overlap any other tile? Possibly there could be a tile covering those rows in other columns, but merging would cause overlap with those other tiles? However, the argument is that we can assume a tiling is \"maximal\" in the sense that you cannot merge left-type tiles with same R without creating overlap. But is that a valid assumption for all tilings? If a tiling is not maximal, you could still have left-type tiles with same R, but then the lower bound argument would not hold. So the lower bound argument may rely on an extra assumption that the tiling is \"maximal\" in some sense, which is not justified. However, perhaps they can argue that we can always merge left-type tiles with same R without violating the condition of at most one tile per unit square, because the rows are disjoint. Let's examine more concretely.\n\nSuppose we have two left-type tiles T1 covering rows i1..i2 and columns L1..R (R same), and T2 covering rows i3..i4 (disjoint intervals) and columns L2..R (L2 maybe > L1). Since they are disjoint rows, they occupy disjoint sets of cells (different rows). If we merge them into a single rectangle covering rows i1..i4 and columns min(L1,L2) .. R, then the rectangle would cover all rows from i1 to i4, including rows between i2+1 and i3-1 (if any). In those rows, we might be covering cells that were previously uncovered (by any tile). But those rows have holes somewhere to the right of column R (since they are left-type). So covering columns up to R does not cover the hole. So the merged rectangle would still be left-type. However, the merged rectangle might overlap with other tiles that previously covered those rows in columns beyond R? Actually other tiles covering those rows might be right-type tiles that occupy columns to the right of the hole. Since the hole is to the right of column R, right-type tiles would occupy columns to the left of the hole? Wait, right-type tiles are defined as those where holes are to the left of the tile. So for a row i, if the hole is to the left of the tile, the tile occupies columns > hole. So for rows where the hole is to the right of column R (i.e., pi(i) > R), there could be a right-type tile covering columns > pi(i), which would be to the right of column R as well. So the merged rectangle covering columns up to R would not overlap with those right-type tiles because they occupy columns > pi(i) > R. So merging left-type tiles with same R does not cause overlap with right-type tiles. So indeed we could merge them and still have a valid tiling with fewer left-type tiles. So any optimal tiling (with minimal number of tiles) would not have two left-type tiles with same R, because you could merge them and reduce tile count, contradicting minimality. However, the lower bound argument is for any admissible tiling, not necessarily minimal. But we can argue that if a tiling has two left-type tiles with same R, we can merge them to get another tiling with strictly fewer tiles, which would still satisfy the constraints (since merging does not create overlap). Therefore, any minimal tiling (i.e., one achieving the minimum number of tiles) must have distinct R values for left-type tiles. But the lower bound argument is for any tiling, not just minimal. However, to prove a lower bound on the minimum number of tiles, it's sufficient to consider minimal tilings, because any tiling with fewer tiles would be minimal. So we can restrict to minimal tilings. The solution didn't explicitly state that they consider a minimal tiling, but they said \"maximality of a tiling\". That may be ambiguous but essentially they are assuming we can consider a tiling that cannot be merged further. That's a reasonable assumption for a minimal tiling. So the lower bound argument seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to verify the lower bound argument: They claim at least n-1 left-type tiles and at least n-1 right-type tiles, thus at least 2n-2 tiles. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nGiven a permutation pi (bijection) representing uncovered squares. For each column c (1..n-1), they claim there must be at least one left-type tile with rightmost column exactly c. Reason: For column c, consider cells (i,c) such that pi(i) > c (i.e., holes to the right of column c). Those cells must be covered, and they can be covered only by a left-type tile whose rightmost column is exactly c. Why cannot a left-type tile with rightmost column > c cover them? Because a left-type tile's rightmost column is the same for all rows it covers; but if its rightmost column > c, then it would also cover column c+1, etc. But the cell (i,c) is in column c; a left-type tile with rightmost column > c could also cover column c, because its column interval includes columns up to its rightmost column. However, the argument says that each column c must have a left-type tile with rightmost column exactly c to cover those cells below the hole. Let's examine.\n\nDefine left-type tile T: For each row i in its row interval, pi(i) > R(T). So holes are to the right of the tile. So the tile occupies columns from L(T) to R(T). For a given column c, consider all rows i where pi(i) > c (hole to the right of column c). For each such row i, the cell (i,c) must be covered. It could be covered either by a left-type tile with R(T) >= c (since the tile includes column c) or by a right-type tile? Right-type tiles have holes to the left, so for those rows i where pi(i) > c, the hole is to the right, so right-type tiles would have holes to the left of the tile, meaning the tile occupies columns > pi(i). Since pi(i) > c, the tile would occupy columns > pi(i) > c, so it would not cover column c. So right-type tiles cannot cover column c for those rows. So the only possible covering for cell (i,c) is a left-type tile whose column interval includes column c. However, the argument says that the left-type tile covering (i,c) must have R(T) = c. But could a left-type tile have R(T) > c and still cover (i,c)? Yes, because its column interval includes columns up to R(T), which includes c. But then the tile would also cover column c+1, etc. But that may be okay. However, the argument wants to guarantee at least one left-type tile with rightmost column exactly c. Let's think: Suppose we have a left-type tile with R(T) = d > c. Then it covers column c for rows in its row interval. But then column c's cells for rows not in that tile's row interval must be covered by some other left-type tile. Could it be that all rows i with pi(i) > c are covered by a single left-type tile with R(T) > c? Possibly, if that tile's row interval includes all those rows. But is that possible? Let's examine the shape of pi: pi is a permutation. For column c, the rows i such that pi(i) > c are those rows whose hole is to the right of column c. This set of rows is not necessarily contiguous; it could be any subset. For example, pi could be identity, then rows i > c have pi(i) = i > c, so rows i > c are exactly those rows > c. That's contiguous (i = c+1,...,n). So a left-type tile covering rows c+1...n and columns up to c (R=c) works. But could we have a left-type tile covering rows c+1...n and columns up to d > c? That tile would also cover column c for those rows, but also cover columns c+1,...,d for those rows. However, those columns may be needed for other rows. But the tile's rightmost column is d, which would be > c. Then the tile would also cover cells (i,c+1) for rows i in its row interval. But those cells might be needed to be covered by some other tile for rows where pi(i) > c+1. This could cause overlapping with other left-type tiles? Let's examine.\n\nIf we have a left-type tile with rightmost column d > c, then for rows i in its row interval, the tile covers columns up to d. For column c+1, those rows also have cells covered by that tile. But those rows also have holes pi(i) > d? Actually for those rows, pi(i) > R(T) = d, because left-type tile requires holes to the right of the tile. So pi(i) > d > c+1. So those rows also have holes to the right of column c+1. So column c+1's cells for those rows are also covered by the same tile. So a left-type tile with R(T) = d > c can cover all cells (i,c) for rows i in its interval, and also covers cells (i,c+1) for those rows. So we could have a left-type tile with R(T) = n-1 covering all rows i > something. But the argument that we need a distinct left-type tile for each column c seems suspect.\n\nLet's test with the identity permutation pi(i)=i. The solution's construction uses left-type tiles V_k for each column k (k=1..n-1). Each V_k is a vertical tile covering column k rows > k (i.e., rows k+1..n). So indeed there is a left-type tile with rightmost column exactly k for each k. So they achieve n-1 left-type tiles.\n\nBut is it necessary that any tiling must have at least n-1 left-type tiles? Could there be a tiling with fewer left-type tiles but still covering all cells? Let's try to think: Suppose we try to cover all cells below the diagonal (i>j) using a single left-type tile that covers many columns. For identity permutation, cells below diagonal are those with i>j. Could we cover them with a single left-type tile? A left-type tile must have holes to the right of its rightmost column. For identity permutation, the holes are at (i,i). For any left-type tile covering rows i in some interval, its rightmost column must be less than the hole column for each row. So if we try to cover rows i from a to b with a tile that covers columns from L to R, we need for each row i in [a,b] that i > R (hole column i > R). So R must be less than the smallest row index in the interval. So we can choose R = a-1 (or less). Then the tile would cover columns up to R, which is less than a. So the tile would be a vertical rectangle covering columns up to R for rows a..b. But to cover all cells below diagonal, we need to cover all columns j < i for each row i. For row a, we need to cover columns 1..a-1. So we could use a tile covering columns 1..a-1 for rows a..b. That would be a rectangle of size (b-a+1)×(a-1). That covers all cells (i,j) where i ∈ [a,b] and j ≤ a-1. But for rows i > a, there are cells with j between a and i-1 that are not covered. So we would need additional tiles. So indeed we need a left-type tile for each column j to cover cells (i,j) with i > j. Because each column j's cells below the diagonal (i>j) need to be covered, and they belong to rows i>j. Could we cover column j's cells (i>j) using a left-type tile that also covers column j+1? That would require the tile's rightmost column to be at least j+1, but then for rows i where i > j+1, the tile would cover column j+1 as well, which may be okay. But then column j's cells for rows i where i ≤ j+1 would not be covered? Actually for row i = j+1, column j is below diagonal (since i > j). That row i = j+1 is in the tile's row interval if we include it. The tile's column interval includes columns up to R, which includes column j. So (j+1, j) is covered. So maybe a left-type tile with rightmost column > j can cover column j's cells for rows in its interval. But then column j+1's cells for those rows would also be covered, which might be okay. But we need to ensure that for each column c, all rows i > c are covered. If we have a left-type tile covering rows from a to b and columns up to R, then for each column c ≤ R, the rows i ∈ [a,b] with i > c are covered. For columns c > R, those rows are not covered by this tile. So to cover column c > R, we need other tiles. So the minimal number of left-type tiles might be less than n-1 if we can use tiles with larger R that cover multiple columns at once. However, the argument that each left-type tile must have distinct R values ensures that each column c must be the rightmost column of some left-type tile. But if we have a left-type tile with rightmost column R = n-1, it would be the rightmost column for all columns up to n-1? Actually the tile's rightmost column is n-1, so it covers columns up to n-1. But the argument says distinct left-type tiles have distinct R values, so we cannot have two left-type tiles with same R. But we could have a left-type tile with R = n-1, covering columns up to n-1, and no other left-type tile with R = n-2, etc. However, does that tile cover all cells below diagonal? Let's test with identity permutation: tile with R = n-1, covering rows a..b (some interval). It would cover columns 1..n-1 for those rows. But for rows i ≤ n-1, the hole is at column i, which is ≤ n-1. For row i in the tile's interval, we need i > R = n-1 to have hole to the right of tile. But i ≤ n-1 cannot be > n-1. So the tile cannot be left-type for rows i ≤ n-1 because the hole column i is not > n-1. So left-type tile with R = n-1 can only cover rows i > n-1, i.e., i = n. So it can only cover row n. That would be a tile covering column 1..n-1 in row n (i.e., a horizontal tile). Wait left-type tile is defined as holes to the right of tile, so for row n, hole is at column n (since pi(n)=n). The tile's rightmost column is n-1, so hole column n > n-1, okay. So left-type tile with R = n-1 can cover row n across columns 1..n-1. That's a horizontal tile? Actually left-type tile can be any shape, but if it covers rows a..b, columns L..R, then for each row i in [a,b], hole column pi(i) > R. So for identity permutation, pi(i)=i, so we need i > R for each i in the interval. So the interval must consist of rows i > R. So if R = n-1, the interval can only include row n (i = n). So left-type tile with R = n-1 can only be a horizontal tile covering row n columns 1..n-1 (size 1×(n-1)). That's indeed a horizontal tile, but they classify horizontal tiles as right-type? Wait they defined left-type as holes to the right of tile. For row n, hole at column n, tile covering columns 1..n-1 is left-type. So that tile is left-type. But they also have right-type tiles defined symmetrically (holes to the left). In the identity permutation, horizontal tiles covering row i columns i+1..n are right-type (holes to the left). Indeed they define horizontal tiles H_k covering row k columns > k, which have holes to the left (since hole at (k,k) is left of tile). So those are right-type. So left-type tiles are vertical tiles V_k covering column k rows > k, holes to the right. So indeed left-type tiles have R = k (the column index). So each left-type tile has distinct R = column index. So they have n-1 left-type tiles.\n\nNow the lower bound argument says any tiling must have at least n-1 left-type tiles. Let's test if we can have a tiling with fewer left-type tiles. Suppose we try to use a left-type tile covering column 1 rows 2..n (V_1). That's one left-type tile. For column 2, we need to cover cells (i,2) for rows i > 2. Could we cover them using the same left-type tile V_1? No, because V_1 only covers column 1. So we need a tile covering column 2. Could we use a left-type tile that covers columns 1 and 2 simultaneously? That would be a rectangle covering columns 1..2 and rows something. But then for rows i in that interval, the hole column i must be > 2. So rows i must be > 2. So we could have a left-type tile covering rows 3..n and columns 1..2. That would cover column 2 cells for rows 3..n (i>2). It would also cover column 1 cells for rows 3..n, but those are already covered by V_1 for rows 3..n? Actually V_1 covers column 1 rows 2..n. So overlapping rows 3..n would cause overlap. So we cannot have that. So we cannot combine columns 1 and 2 in a single left-type tile without overlapping with V_1. Could we instead use a left-type tile covering rows 2..n and columns 1..2? That would cover column 1 rows 2..n (including rows 2..n) and column 2 rows 2..n. But then row 2's hole is at column 2 (since identity). For row 2, hole column is 2, which is not > R=2 (hole is not to the right). So left-type tile cannot cover row 2 because hole is not to the right. So row 2 cannot be included. So left-type tile covering rows 3..n and columns 1..2 is possible, but overlapping with V_1 for column 1 rows 3..n. So we cannot combine columns.\n\nThus indeed we need separate left-type tiles for each column. So the lower bound seems plausible.\n\nNow for right-type tiles, similarly we need one per row.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 holds.\n\nNow the construction uses exactly 2n-2 tiles, achieving the bound.\n\nThus the solution appears correct.\n\nNow we need to follow the hierarchical decision tree.\n\nLevel 1: Identify final answer: 4048. It's an optimization problem (minimum). According to Level 1, we must not check correctness of answer; only check for arithmetic errors and constraint violations. The answer is computed as 2*2025-2 = 4048, which is correct arithmetic. No constraint violations in the solution. So Level 1 passes, proceed to Level 1.5.\n\nLevel 1.5: Check optimality. The solution provides a lower bound argument and a construction achieving that bound. We need to test small cases (n=3, n=4, n=5) using the proposed construction and see if any alternative approach yields fewer tiles. The construction yields 2n-2 tiles. For n=3, that would be 4 tiles. Let's test if we can tile a 3x3 grid with fewer than 4 tiles while satisfying each row and column has exactly one uncovered cell. Could we do it with 3 tiles? Let's think. For n=3, we need each row and column to have exactly one uncovered cell. So we need exactly 3 uncovered cells (one per row and column). The uncovered cells must be a permutation matrix. So there are 3 uncovered cells. The total number of unit squares is 9. So we need to cover 6 squares with tiles. Tiles are rectangles covering disjoint sets of squares. Could we cover 6 squares with 3 tiles? Possibly, e.g., three 2x1 tiles covering two squares each, total 6 squares. But can we arrange them to avoid covering uncovered cells? Let's try to find a tiling with 3 tiles for n=3. Uncovered cells could be diagonal (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). Then we need to cover all other cells: (1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2). Could we cover them with 3 rectangles? For example, tile covering (1,2)-(1,3) (row 1 columns 2-3) is a 1x2 tile. Tile covering (2,1)-(3,1) (column 1 rows 2-3) is a 2x1 tile. Tile covering (2,3)-(3,2)? That's not a rectangle. Could we cover (2,3) and (3,2) with a 2x2 tile covering rows 2-3 columns 2-3? That would also cover (2,2) and (3,3), which are uncovered cells, not allowed. So can't. Could we cover (2,3) and (3,2) with separate 1x1 tiles? Not allowed because we need to minimize number of tiles, but we could use 1x1 tiles, but they'd increase tile count. So maybe 3 tiles is impossible. The lower bound says at least 2n-2 = 4 tiles. So 4 tiles is minimal. So the construction is optimal for n=3. For n=4, lower bound says at least 6 tiles. Could there be a tiling with 5 tiles? Possibly not. The lower bound argument seems robust. So no alternative approach yields fewer tiles for small n. So optimality check passes.\n\nBut we need to be thorough: The lower bound argument is plausible but we need to ensure there is no hidden flaw that would allow a tiling with fewer than 2n-2 tiles for some n. The solution's lower bound argument is based on classification of left-type and right-type tiles and counting distinct rightmost columns and leftmost rows. This seems correct. However, we must check if the argument uses any hidden assumption like \"maximality of a tiling\" that is not justified. The solution says \"If two distinct left-type tiles had the same value of R, their row intervals could be merged into a single larger rectangle (still left-type), contradicting maximality of a tiling.\" They didn't explicitly define maximality, but they intend that we can assume a tiling that cannot be merged further (i.e., a minimal tiling). This is a reasonable assumption for a minimal tiling. However, the lower bound argument is for any tiling, not just minimal. But the argument uses \"maximality\" to argue distinct R values. If we consider a tiling that is not minimal, it could have two left-type tiles with same R, but then we could merge them to get a tiling with fewer tiles, which would contradict the minimality of the original tiling if we assume we are looking at a minimal tiling. So the lower bound argument is essentially proving that any minimal tiling must have at least n-1 left-type tiles. That's enough to prove that the minimum number of tiles is at least 2n-2. So the lower bound argument is valid.\n\nThus optimality is plausible.\n\nNow Level 2: Check reasoning validity. The solution uses valid mathematical principles: classification of tiles, counting arguments, symmetry, construction, etc. No invalid reasoning. The method is valid.\n\nNow Level 3: Check presentation quality. Are there any justification gaps? The solution is fairly complete. However, we need to examine if there are any missing details or ambiguous statements. The solution defines left-type and right-type tiles, uses arguments about distinct R values, but might not fully justify the claim that each column c must have a left-type tile with rightmost column exactly c. The argument is somewhat informal but seems logically sound: For each column c, the cells (i,c) with pi(i) > c must be covered, and they can only be covered by left-type tiles whose rightmost column is at least c. But they claim exactly c. Let's examine if that step is fully justified. They say: \"For each column c∈{1,…,n-1} the cells of that column that lie below the hole in column c (i.e., the cells (i,c) with π(i)>c) must be covered, and they can be covered only by a left‑type tile whose rightmost column is exactly c (a right‑type tile never reaches column c). Hence there must be at least one left‑type tile for every c=1,…,n-1.\" This is a bit of a jump: they assert that the covering tile must have rightmost column exactly c, not just ≥ c. The justification is that if a left-type tile had rightmost column > c, it would also cover column c for its rows, but also cover column c+1, etc. But maybe those cells could be covered by other tiles; but the argument that there must be a left-type tile with rightmost column exactly c is not fully rigorous. However, this is a presentation gap, not a logical error: the argument can be made rigorous with additional detail. So we can classify this as a Justification Gap (Level 3). The solution also uses the phrase \"maximality of a tiling\" without defining it, which is a minor gap. But overall the reasoning is valid.\n\nThus we have Level 1 passed, Level 1.5 passed (optimality not suspicious), Level 2 passed (valid methods), Level 3 has justification gaps but no critical errors. So final verdict PASS.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON output with fields: Level 1 Result