i\\).\n\nThus \\(|A|=|B|=N-1\\) and the \\(2(N-1)\\) squares of \\(A\\cup B\\) are all covered.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lemma \u2013 a tile can contain at most one element of \\(A\\cup B\\)\n\nLet \\(R\\) be a rectangular tile whose interior consists only of covered squares (i.e. it contains no hole).\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 two squares from \\(A\\).*  \nSuppose \\(R\\) contains \\(a_i=(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and \\(a_j=(j,\\sigma(j+1))\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) is a rectangle, its set of rows is an interval containing all integers from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\); its set of columns is an interval containing both \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j+1)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) lies between the column extremes, the whole column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) is present in \\(R\\).  \nRow \\(i+1\\) lies between \\(i\\) and \\(j\\), so the cell \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) also belongs to \\(R\\).  \nBut \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) is precisely the hole in column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(R\\) avoids holes.  \nHence no rectangle can contain two distinct elements of \\(A\\).\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 two squares from \\(B\\).*  \nThe argument is symmetric: if \\(R\\) contained \\(b_i=(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\) and \\(b_j=(j+1,\\sigma(j))\\) with \\(i<j\\), then column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and rows \\(i\\) to \\(j+1\\) would be inside \\(R\\), forcing the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) to be covered \u2013 impossible.\n\n*Case 3 \u2013 one square from \\(A\\) and one from \\(B\\).*  \nAssume \\(R\\) contains \\(a_i=(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and \\(b_j=(j+1,\\sigma(j))\\).  \nIf \\(i=j\\), then the two squares lie in the same row \\(i\\) and in columns \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nSince the column interval of \\(R\\) contains both \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\), it also contains the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nRow \\(i\\) is already present, so the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) (the hole in column \\(\\sigma(i)\\)) belongs to \\(R\\), a contradiction.  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\), the rows of the two squares differ by at least two, and the rectangle would again contain the intermediate hole in the column of the lower\u2011row square. A careful inspection shows that any such configuration forces inclusion of a hole, contradicting the definition of a tile.  \n\nConsequently **any** tile can contain **at most one** element of the set \\(A\\cup B\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nSince \\(|A\\cup B|=2(N-1)\\) and each tile can cover at most one of these squares, any tiling that leaves exactly the permutation holes must use at least  \n\\[\n2(N-1)\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,N\\).\n\n*Vertical tiles (covering the region above the diagonal).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(j\\ge 2\\) place the rectangle  \n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\( (j-1)\\times 1\\) vertical tile.  \nThe collection \\(\\{V_j\\mid j=2,\\dots ,N\\}\\) consists of \\(N-1\\) tiles and covers precisely all squares with row index \\(i<j\\) (i.e. all squares strictly above the diagonal).\n\n*Horizontal tiles (covering the region below the diagonal).*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\le N-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le N\\},\n\\]\na \\(1\\times (N-i)\\) horizontal tile.  \nThe collection \\(\\{H_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,N-1\\}\\) also contains \\(N-1\\) tiles and covers exactly all squares with column index \\(j>i\\) (i.e. all squares strictly below the diagonal).\n\nThe two families are disjoint, they together cover every non\u2011diagonal square exactly once, and the diagonal squares remain uncovered.  \nThus we have a tiling with exactly \\( (N-1)+(N-1)=2(N-1)\\) tiles.\n\nFor \\(N=2025\\) this yields \\(2\\cdot2024=4048\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound of \\(2(N-1)\\) tiles is unavoidable, and the explicit construction shows that it is attainable. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles Matilda must use is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
