ered unit square. So we need to cover all but n cells (one per row, one per column). Since each uncovered cell is unique per row and column, the uncovered cells form a permutation matrix: exactly one uncovered cell per row and column, i.e., a set of n cells with distinct rows and columns.\n\nWe need to cover all other cells with rectangles (tiles) that do not overlap and cover each cell at most once. We want to minimize the number of tiles.\n\nThe solution claims lower bound of 2n-2 tiles. Let's examine that.\n\nDefine L(T) and R(T) as the leftmost and rightmost column indices of tile T. For each row i that intersects T, the uncovered cell (i, π(i)) cannot be inside T, so either π(i) > R(T) (hole to the right) or π(i) < L(T) (hole to the left). So each tile is either left-type (holes to the right for all rows intersecting it) or right-type (holes to the left). This classification seems plausible: For a tile covering a set of rows, the uncovered cells for those rows must all be on the same side of the tile (all left or all right). Actually, we need to check: Could a tile have some rows where the hole is left and some rows where the hole is right? Let's consider a tile T that covers rows i1,...,ik (contiguous rows, because it's a rectangle). For each row i in that interval, the uncovered column π(i) must be either left of L(T) or right of R(T). If for some row i we have π(i) < L(T) and for another row j we have π(j) > R(T), then the tile would have uncovered cells on both sides, which would mean the uncovered cells are on both sides of the tile. But is that possible? The tile covers columns from L(T) to R(T). If there is a row i where π(i) < L(T) (hole left), and another row j where π(j) > R(T) (hole right), then the tile would be \"sandwiched\" between holes on left and right. But the tile's rows are contiguous, and the holes are at distinct columns. Is there any reason that can't happen? The tile covers a rectangle of columns L(T) to R(T). For each row i in the tile