ntradiction. So the classification into left-type or right-type may be false; a tile could have holes on both sides. But maybe the tile's rows are contiguous, and the holes are distinct columns. If the holes are on both sides, then there would be at least one row where the hole is left of the tile and another row where the hole is right. The tile would then have rows with holes on both sides, but is that allowed? The tile covers the rectangle; the uncovered cells are not covered, so they can be anywhere else. There's no rule forbidding that. So the classification might be flawed.\n\nBut perhaps we can argue that if a tile has holes on both sides, we could split it into two tiles (left and right) to reduce tile count? Actually, we want to minimize number of tiles, so we might want to combine rows with holes on both sides into one tile? Let's examine.\n\nThe lower bound argument uses classification to claim that each left-type tile has a distinct rightmost column R, and each column c from 1 to n-1 must have a left-type tile with R=c. This yields at least n-1 left-type tiles. Similarly for right-type tiles yields at least n-1. This gives lower bound 2n-2.\n\nIf classification is flawed, the lower bound may be invalid. Let's examine the lower bound more carefully.\n\nWe need to cover all cells except the n holes. The holes are at positions (i, π(i)). Tiles are rectangles that cannot cover any hole. So each tile must avoid all holes. For each tile, consider its column interval [L,R] and row interval [U,D] (since it's a rectangle). For each row i in [U,D], the hole in that row is at column π(i). Since the tile covers columns L..R, the hole cannot be in that interval, so π(i) ∉ [L,R] for each i ∈ [U,D]. So for each i in the tile's rows, π(i) is either < L or > R. So the tile's rows are partitioned into those whose holes are left of L and those whose holes are right of R. It's possible that some rows have holes left and some have holes right. So the tile could be \"mixed ty