d those. Could we use a tile covering (1,2),(2,2) is not allowed. So any tile that includes a diagonal cell is forbidden. So we need to cover cells that are off-diagonal. The off-diagonal cells are arranged in two triangles: below diagonal: (2,1),(3,1),(3,2); above diagonal: (1,2),(1,3),(2,3). The solution uses vertical tiles for each column below diagonal and horizontal tiles for each row above diagonal. Could we combine some of them? For example, we could cover (2,1),(3,1),(3,2) with a 2x2 tile covering rows 2-3, columns 1-2, but that includes (2,2) which is diagonal (uncovered). So not allowed. Could we cover (2,1),(3,1) with a vertical tile (as in solution) and (3,2) with a 1x1 tile? That would be 3 tiles for lower triangle, plus maybe 2 tiles for upper triangle? That would be 5 tiles. So 4 seems minimal. So lower bound holds for n=3.\n\nFor n=4, solution gives 6 tiles. Could there be a tiling with 5 tiles? Let's try to see if lower bound 2n-2 = 6 is minimal. The lower bound argument may be correct for small n. However, we need to verify the classification argument for general n.\n\nWe need to see if there is any tiling with fewer than 2n-2 tiles for any n. The solution's lower bound argument is plausible but may have a flaw. Let's examine the argument in detail.\n\nDefine left-type tile: all rows intersecting tile have holes to the right (π(i) > R(T)). Right-type tile: all rows intersecting tile have holes to the left (π(i) < L(T)). They claim each tile must be either left-type or right-type. As argued, this may not be forced. However, perhaps we can argue that if a tile had both left and right holes, then there would be a row i with π(i) < L and a row j with π(j) > R. Then the tile's rows interval includes both i and j. The holes are on opposite sides. Then consider the column of the hole left of the tile (say column c = π(i) < L). That column's hole is at row i, which is within the tile's rows. Similarly, column d = π(j) > R has hole at row j within tile's ro