e condition that each column also has exactly one uncovered square. Could that force the holes to be all on one side? Let's analyze.\n\nWe have a rectangle covering rows I = [a,b] and columns J = [c,d]. For each row i in I, the uncovered column pi(i) is not in J. So pi(i) ∈ [1,c-1] ∪ [d+1,n]. Could it be that for some i, pi(i) < c, and for some j, pi(j) > d? Yes, as we saw. Does that cause any column to have more than one uncovered square? Let's check columns left of c: any column ℓ < c can have uncovered square at some row i ∈ I (if pi(i)=ℓ). Similarly, columns right of d can have uncovered squares at rows in I. So it's possible that both left and right columns get uncovered squares from rows in I. That seems allowed as long as each column gets exactly one uncovered square overall. So classification into left-type or right-type is not necessarily forced. However, the solution's argument for lower bound uses that each tile is either left-type or right-type, and then counts distinct rightmost columns for left-type tiles and distinct leftmost rows for right-type tiles. If classification is wrong, the lower bound may be invalid.\n\nBut perhaps the solution's classification is correct under the condition that the tiles are maximal (cannot be extended). They mention \"maximality of a tiling\" when merging two left-type tiles with same R. But the classification might still be flawed. Let's examine the lower bound more carefully.\n\nWe need to find the minimum number of rectangles covering all cells except exactly one per row and column. The known answer is indeed 2n - 2. This is a known problem: \"minimum number of rectangles needed to cover all cells of an n×n board except a permutation matrix\" is 2n - 2. The solution's lower bound is standard: each rectangle can be either \"horizontal\" or \"vertical\" relative to the permutation matrix. Actually, typical solution: For each column except the column of the uncovered square in the last row, you need a vertical rectangle 